# Copy to .env and fill in credentials from a Google Cloud desktop OAuth client.
# Enable Google Drive API, YouTube Data API, YouTube Analytics API, and
# YouTube Reporting API for that project.
#
# `login` opens two consent screens. Google rejects Drive scopes and YouTube
# scopes in one request, so they are never combined and include_granted_scopes
# is not set. Re-run login after upgrading; a token from the old single consent
# is not refreshed.
# Step 1 of 2: Google Drive, with the account that owns the Drive folders.
#   https://www.googleapis.com/auth/drive.readonly
#   https://www.googleapis.com/auth/drive.file
# drive.readonly reads the story inbox and finds an existing stats file.
# drive.file creates uploads.jsonl and overwrites the stats CSV. It does not
# edit story videos.
# Step 2 of 2: YouTube, with the account that owns the channel.
#   https://www.googleapis.com/auth/youtube.force-ssl
#   https://www.googleapis.com/auth/yt-analytics.readonly
# youtube.force-ssl uploads and reads the channel.
# yt-analytics.readonly reads YouTube Analytics reports and YouTube Reporting API
# bulk reports (thumbnail impressions and click-through rate).
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Drive token. YouTube credentials are saved beside this file by default.
GOOGLE_TOKEN_PATH=secrets/token.json

# Optional. When unset, the YouTube token is GOOGLE_TOKEN_PATH with ".youtube"
# before the extension (secrets/token.json -> secrets/token.youtube.json).
# GOOGLE_YOUTUBE_TOKEN_PATH=secrets/token.youtube.json

# The upload log is not a secret. Set upload_log_folder_id in config.json
# (see examples/config.example.json). The app creates uploads.jsonl in that
# folder. No new environment variable is required. After login saves both
# tokens, a pending log line is retried.
